{{also|treehugger|tree-hugger}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|treehugger|tree-hugger}}

===Etymology===
Compound of {{com|en|tree|hugger}}. Popularized after the 1970's [[Chipko]] movement in India, who resorted to actual group hugging of trees in order to prevent deforestation. {{w|Tommy James}} claims that his 1971 song ''Draggin' the Line'' popularized the phrase.<ref>{{cite-web|url=https://americansongwriter.com/meaning-behind-tommy-james-draggin-the-line/|title=Meaning Behind Tommy James’ “Draggin’ the Line”|author=Alli Patton|date=July 5, 2023|work=American Songwriter}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-tree hugger.ogg|a=AU}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slang|pejorative}} An [[environmental]] [[campaigner]], especially one who aims to [[restrict]] [[logging]] and especially one who uses dramatic, attention-grabbing methods of obstruction.
#: {{cot|en|bunny hugger}}
#* '''1999''', ''Mother Jones Magazine'' (Mar–Apr 1999, page 64)
#*: Animal liberationists like Rosmarino resented the '''tree huggers'''' insensitivity to animals and reluctance to risk jail time, while more mainstream environmentalists like AFR's Jeff Berman disagreed with the bunny huggers' wacky morality and in-your-face tactics. The two groups' differences were in full view as they teamed up to parade through Vail last winter.
# {{lb|en|slang}} A [[hippie]].{{rfex|en}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005| title=Miss Diagnosed: Unraveling Chronic Stress|author=Erin Bell | page=| passage=The idea of naturopathic doctors had always formed an image in my mind of old, bearded, '''tree-huggers''' who boiled roots and made poultices out of gross things to treat weird people who believed in that "natural" stuff.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014| title=The Road of Darkness|author=Zoe Ambler | page=| passage=She'd heard the term from American soldiers about these 'free love' people. ''''Tree huggers'''' who reveled in sex, drink and drugs.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020| title=Simple Spirituality|author=D. L. Kline | page=| passage=Until fairly recently, even the idea of a MBS connection was dismissed by Western medicine as the province of New Age '''tree huggers''', so it had very little mainstream credibility. }}

